{{smaller block|<poem>No more fool,
To look gay ''babies in your eyes'', young Roland,
And hang about your pretty neck.</poem>
{{right|''Fletcher'', Woman’s Prize, v. 1.}}}}
{{smaller block|But wee cannot so passe the centre of the Eye, which wee call Pupilla, quasi Puppa, the ''babie in the eye'', the Sight. {{right|''Purchas'', Microcos. (1619), p. 90.}}}}
'''4.''' One who is like a baby; a childish person.
{{smaller block|Though he be grave with years, he’s a great ''baby''.
{{right|''Fletcher (and another)'', Elder Brother, iii. 5.}}}}

{{smaller|'''Baby act''', a colloquial name for the legal defense of infancy. Hence—'''To plead the baby act.''' (''a'') To plead that a contract is void because made during one’s minority. (''b'') To attempt to excuse excessive or feigned ignorance or stupidity on the ground of professed inexperience. [Colloquial in both uses.]—'''Bartholomew baby''', a kind of doll sold originally at Bartholomew fair in London, and celebrated as the best then known.}}
{{smaller block|It also tells farmers what manner of wife they shall choose; not one trickt up with ribbens and knots like a ''Bartholomew baby''. {{right|''Poor Robin’s Almanac'', 1695.}}}}
'''II.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Babyish; infantine; pertaining to an infant.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ye conscience-stricken cravens, rock to rest
Your ''baby'' hearts. {{right|''Shelley'', The Cenci, iv. 2.}}</poem>}}
{{smaller block|Moulded thy ''baby'' thought. {{right|''Tennyson'', Eleänore.}}}}
'''2.''' Small, or comparatively small: as, a ''baby'' engine. [Colloq.]

'''baby''' (bā′bi), ''v. t.''; pret. and pp. ''babied'', ppr. ''babying''. [< ''baby'', ''n.''] To treat like a young child.
{{smaller block|<poem>At best it ''babies'' us with endless toys,
And keeps us children till we drop to dust.</poem>
{{right|''Young'', Night Thoughts, vi. 521.}}}}

