Mistook the bliss of heaven for ''bacchanals'' above.
{{right|''Dryden'', Hind and Panther, i. 387.}}}}

Also ''bacchanalian.''

'''bacchanalia''' (bak-a-nā′liạ), ''n. pl.'' [L. (OL. ''bacanalia''), neut. pl. of ''bacchanalis'', pertaining to Bacchus: see ''bacchanal.''] '''1.''' [''cap.''] In ''Rom. antiq.'', a festival in honor of Bacchus. These festivals became the occasion of great excesses, and were forbidden by the senate in 186 {{sc|b. c.}} —'''2.''' Any festivities characterized by jollity and good-fellowship, particularly if somewhat boisterous, and accompanied by much wine-drinking.
{{smaller block|The morning after the ''bacchanalia'' in the saloon of the palace.
{{right|''L. Wallace'', Ben-Hur, p. 283.}}}}
'''3.''' Drunken orgies; riotous disorders; ruthless and shameless excesses; unbounded license.
{{smaller block|Plunging without restraint or shame into the ''Bacchanalia'' of despotism, the king [John] continued to pillage, to banish, and to slay. ''Sir E. Creasy'', Eng. Const., p. 110.}}

'''bacchanalian''' (bak-ạ-nā′liạn), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''bacchanalia'' + ''-an.''] Same as ''bacchanal.'' [The more common form of the adjective.]
{{smaller block|Ev’n ''bacchanalian'' madness has its charms.
{{right|''Cowper'', Progress of Error, l. 56.}}
Sculptures of the ''bacchanalians.'' ''Stukeley.''}}

'''bacchanalianism''' (bak-a-nā′liạn-izm), ''n.'' [< ''bacchanalian'' + ''-ism.''] The practice of bacchanalian rites; drunken revelry; riotous festivity.

'''bacchanalianly''' (bak-ạ-nā′liạn-li), ''adv.'' In a bacchanalian manner.

